{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JMTKLFWjcKxPvaJbw63AWB","type":"tool_result","content":"**Proposition 1 (Prop. 1)**, California General Election, November 3, 2026\n\n- **Official title:** \"AUTHORIZES BONDS FOR HOUSING AFFORDABILITY PROGRAMS. LEGISLATIVE STATUTE.\" The page shows no separate ballot label, so this heading is the closest match. The Legislature placed the measure before voters.\n- **Official summary:** It opens with \"Authorizes $11.25 billion in state general obligation bonds,\" for affordable-housing programs, including rental housing in multi-unit buildings, home loans for veterans, \"supportive housing,\" preserving existing affordable homes, and down payment help.\n- **Fiscal impact:** The state's costs would rise by $500 million to $600 million per year for around 25 years to pay back the borrowing.\n- **YES vote:** Would let the state take on $11.25 billion in borrowing for veterans' and affordable-housing programs.\n- **NO vote:** Would block the state from borrowing that $11.25 billion for these purposes.\n- **Arguments for (supporters' claims):** Supporters say it would create affordable housing for veterans, older adults, people with disabilities, working families with lower and middle incomes, and essential workers such as educators, firefighters, and nurses. They also say it would widen homeownership, speed up affordable projects ready to start construction, and help people at risk of becoming homeless. They add that it sets financial accountability rules and public monitoring, and does not raise taxes.\n- **Arguments against:** None were submitted, and no opponents are listed.\n- **Supporters:** US Vets; CA Federation of Teachers; Self-Help for the Elderly; Habitat for Humanity CA; CA Alliance of Child & Family Services\n- **Supporter contact:** yesonprop1ca.com or info@yesonprop1ca.com\n- **Key dates:** Counties begin mailing ballots by October 5, 2026. The voter registration deadline is October 19, 2026. Election Day is November 3, 2026, with polls open 7:00 a.m. to 8:00 p.m.\n- **Other:** The page links to a PDF of the full proposed law."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d980b4f3-b11e-4a67-8850-bbda45ad8508","uuid":"d06920ce-1e59-4d69-869d-771418015171","timestamp":"2026-11-03T11:10:22.549Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":13988,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Proposition 1 (Prop. 1)**, California General Election, November 3, 2026\n\n- **Official title:** \"AUTHORIZES BONDS FOR HOUSING AFFORDABILITY PROGRAMS. LEGISLATIVE STATUTE.\" The page shows no separate ballot label, so this heading is the closest match. The Legislature placed the measure before voters.\n- **Official summary:** It opens with \"Authorizes $11.25 billion in state general obligation bonds,\" for affordable-housing programs, including rental housing in multi-unit buildings, home loans for veterans, \"supportive housing,\" preserving existing affordable homes, and down payment help.\n- **Fiscal impact:** The state's costs would rise by $500 million to $600 million per year for around 25 years to pay back the borrowing.\n- **YES vote:** Would let the state take on $11.25 billion in borrowing for veterans' and affordable-housing programs.\n- **NO vote:** Would block the state from borrowing that $11.25 billion for these purposes.\n- **Arguments for (supporters' claims):** Supporters say it would create affordable housing for veterans, older adults, people with disabilities, working families with lower and middle incomes, and essential workers such as educators, firefighters, and nurses. They also say it would widen homeownership, speed up affordable projects ready to start construction, and help people at risk of becoming homeless. They add that it sets financial accountability rules and public monitoring, and does not raise taxes.\n- **Arguments against:** None were submitted, and no opponents are listed.\n- **Supporters:** US Vets; CA Federation of Teachers; Self-Help for the Elderly; Habitat for Humanity CA; CA Alliance of Child & Family Services\n- **Supporter contact:** yesonprop1ca.com or info@yesonprop1ca.com\n- **Key dates:** Counties begin mailing ballots by October 5, 2026. The voter registration deadline is October 19, 2026. Election Day is November 3, 2026, with polls open 7:00 a.m. to 8:00 p.m.\n- **Other:** The page links to a PDF of the full proposed law.","durationMs":112894,"url":"https://voterguide.sos.ca.gov/propositions/1/"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JMTKLFWjcKxPvaJbw63AWB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
